// tap-echo: --buffer 1024
// tolerance_usecs: 4000
// Zhida's own receive window closes. tap-echo gives each connection 1024-byte buffers;
// the SYN-ACK's window 1024 and wscale 0 show the flag reached the EchoServer. The Linux
// client keeps its window at 0, so no echo can leave: its first 1024 bytes fill Zhida's
// tx buffer, and every later segment stays unread, holding one RX frame. The window counts
// free RX frames (1024-byte buffer: 6 frames), capped at the 1024-byte buffer, not unread
// bytes: it stays 1024 until the sixth unread segment takes the last frame, and then Zhida
// advertises window 0. Zhida answers the client's zero-window probe with an ACK of window
// 0. When the client opens its window, the echo drains one segment per client ACK, and the
// first segment after a frame is freed carries the reopened window. Handshake: see
// handshake-linux-options.pkt. smoltcp 0.14.0 src/socket/tcp.rs:
// - SYN-ACK: window min(rx window, 65535) unscaled; shift 0 because bit_length(1024) is
//   under 16; wscale echoed since the SYN has it.
// - First 1024 bytes: they consume the advertised receive window. EchoServer::step
//   reads them and queues the echo; window_to_update sends a pure ACK at once with
//   window 1024, even though the 10 ms delayed-ACK timer is still waiting. Linux v6.12
//   tcp_receive_window subtracts bytes received since the last ACK; __tcp_cleanup_rbuf
//   sends an ACK when a read raises that remaining window at least twofold (here 0 to
//   1024), independently of quick-ACK mode:
//   https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.12/include/net/tcp.h#L813-L820
//   https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.12/net/ipv4/tcp.c#L1480-L1505
//   The same dispatch arms a probe for one RTO later, 1.5 s (1.2 s + 300 ms); see
//   peer-zero-window.pkt, whose larger receive window does not trigger an immediate update.
//   The client opens its window at 1.46 s, before the probe is due, so no Zhida probe
//   leaves.
// - Next five 1024-byte segments: step cannot move them (no tx space, crates/tap-echo/src/
//   lib.rs step), so each holds a frame. Each consumes the whole advertised window, and the
//   free frames still allow 1024, a raise from 0, so window_to_update ACKs each at once
//   with window 1024 (the fifth leaves one free frame, 1460 B, still capped at 1024).
// - Sixth: it takes the last frame; the window stays 0, so only the 10 ms delayed ACK
//   goes out, window 0.
// - The client's probe is Linux's: no payload, sequence number one below SND.UNA. smoltcp
//   treats a zero-length segment just left of the window as a keep-alive or window probe
//   and answers with a challenge ACK, limited to one per 500 ms (this is the connection's
//   first): SND.NXT, RCV.NXT, window 0.
// - The client's window update stops Zhida's probe timer and the same turn sends the
//   first echo segment, still window 0 (no free frame). Each client ACK frees the tx
//   buffer; step moves the next unread segment into it and drops its frame, and the next
//   turn sends it. Sending data comes first, so the reopened window rides on the data
//   segments: 1024 (one or more free frames, capped at the buffer).

0     < S 0:0(0) win 64240 <mss 1460,nop,nop,sackOK,nop,wscale 7>
+0    > S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 1024 <mss 1460,wscale 0,sackOK,eol,eol,eol>
+0    < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 0
+1.2  < P. 1:1025(1024) ack 1 win 0
+0    > . 1:1(0) ack 1025 win 1024
+0    < . 1025:1025(0) ack 1 win 0
1.3   < P. 1025:2049(1024) ack 1 win 0
